    <hal format="hidl">
        <!-- KEYS ON THIS DEVICE ARE NOT TEE-BACKED. This entry reads like an
             ordinary hardware HAL and is not one: the service behind it is
             AOSP's own hardware/interfaces/keymaster/3.0/default, built from
             source by device.mk, and its createKeymaster3Device()
             (KeymasterDevice.cpp:67-75) calls hw_get_module_by_class for
             KEYSTORE_HARDWARE_MODULE_ID, logs "Could not find any keystore
             module, using software-only implementation." and returns
             CreateKeymasterDevice(), the pure-software SoftKeymaster.

             There is no keystore.*.so in vendor/xsh/k50sv1_64_bsp to find, and
             none in the stock image either: stock's vendor/lib64/hw carries
             android.hardware.keymaster@3.0-impl.so and no keystore module, so
             Stock ran the same software implementation. This is a property of
             the handset, not a regression introduced by this port.

             Consequence: KM_TAG_ROOT_OF_TRUST, hardware-enforced key
             authorisations and attestation are all software claims. Anything
             that must be genuinely hardware-bound has to look elsewhere. The
             gatekeeper HAL is the same story, which is why extract-files.sh
             pins libSoftGatekeeper.so by hash rather than the
             gatekeeper.default.so symlink to it. -->
        <name>android.hardware.keymaster</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <fqname>@3.0::IKeymasterDevice/default</fqname>
    </hal>
