    <!-- wpa_supplicant registers MediaTek's own extension interface alongside
         the three AOSP ones (confirmed on the handset: all four are served by
         /vendor/bin/hw/wpa_supplicant). Without this entry hwservicemanager has
         no transport for it, so a getService() would fall through to passthrough
         and fail. Nothing on a pure-AOSP Wi-Fi framework calls it today; it is
         declared because the process really does serve it, and an undeclared
         registered interface is a latent trap.
         sepolicy/vendor/hwservice_contexts already labels it
         hal_wifi_supplicant_hwservice.

         The audio service keeps its existing installed filename,
         android.hardware.audio@5.0-service-mediatek, and now compiles AOSP's
         multi-version service.cpp. It first registers Audio 5.0 and Effects
         5.0, and also registers Bluetooth Audio Provider 2.0, as declared
         above. Its optional SoundTrigger 2.x and legacy Bluetooth A2DP
         offload 1.0 registration attempts do not establish device support:
         no SoundTrigger feature or A2DP hardware-offload property is enabled.
         These optional interfaces remain undeclared. The AOSP service does
         not link the unused BroadcastRadio libraries from the old binary. -->
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>vendor.mediatek.hardware.wifi.supplicant</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <fqname>@2.0::ISupplicant/default</fqname>
    </hal>

